---
// Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor. Workshop
// slides for ATypI 2026 Sharjah. Part 1 is a short demo. Part 2 is
// hands-on setup on the attendees' own machines: hardware, installation,
// setup with a coding agent, and exercises from the demo repository.
// Companion to runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html and the demo repo.
import Fig from '@/components/slides/Fig.astro'
import Slide from '@/components/slides/Slide.astro'
import SlidesLayout from '@/layouts/SlidesLayout.astro'

import endRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/end-runebender.jpg'
import grid from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-grid.png'
import nodes from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nodes.png'
import nufoEditing from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-nufo-editing.png'
import outline from '@/assets/slides/localai/runebender-xilem-outline.png'
import titleRunebender from '@/assets/slides/localai/title-runebender.jpg'
---

<SlidesLayout
  title="Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor (slides)"
  description="Workshop slides for Local AI for Type Design with the Runebender Font Editor, ATypI 2026 Sharjah."
  running="Local AI for Type Design"
  author="Eli Heuer"
>
  <!-- ── Opening ── -->

  <Slide label="Title">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={titleRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a bismillah labeled for training: each letter’s ink inside a colored polygon."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <h1 class="t-display descends">Local AI for<br />Type Design</h1>
      <p class="t-head">with the Runebender Font Editor</p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-1-6 over-bleed at-bottom">
      <p class="t-body">Eli Heuer · ATypI 2026 Sharjah</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Runebender is an open-source, cross-platform font editor written in Rust. It runs, trains, and fine-tunes models on your own machine.</p>
      <p>Setup instructions are at runebender.org/docs/local-ai-setup.html. The exercises are in the runebender-local-ai-demo repository.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Overview" label="Format">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Format</h2>
      <p class="t-body">
        Part 1 is a short demo. It shows Runebender training and fine-tuning
        small models, running open-weight models, and building a workflow in
        the node editor, on Mac and Linux.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Part 2 is hands-on. We set up Runebender and local models on your own
        machine: from a DMG on a Mac, with a few terminal commands on Mac or
        Linux, or with a coding agent.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        After setup, no account or network connection is needed. In
        Runebender, a model is a folder of files on your disk.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A Runebender model folder holds three or four files: a config, the weights, a vocabulary, and sometimes a manifest.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Why local ── -->

  <Slide section="Why local" label="Why local">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Why local</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Small models run on a laptop. A PC with a good graphics card can
        train larger ones.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The designer controls each part of the process: the training data,
        the model files, and what reaches the font. The editor downloads
        nothing and sends nothing.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The font models are one to a few dozen megabytes. Training one from
        scratch takes minutes on a laptop GPU.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={nufoEditing}
      alt="Runebender editing Arabic glyphs with a rendered preview."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Most font generation research trains on large collections of scraped fonts, which mix many drawing conventions. These models train on the designer’s own drawings, made on one grid.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why local" label="The training loop">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">The training loop</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The designer draws a core set of glyphs. The editor drafts the rest.
        The designer reviews and corrects the drafts. Each accepted draft is
        added to the training data.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The designer reviews shapes. Recurring errors get a detector or a
        repair tool before the next training run.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Draw and grade</td><td>A reference set in one master, marked green</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Train</td><td>The model trains on the green set only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Draft</td><td>10 to 20 glyphs on the 8-unit grid, as a proposal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Review and fix</td><td>Correct, install, mark orange</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Grade</td><td>Corrected drafts are marked green and join the set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Build</td><td>Compile the font and proof it in text</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Diagnose</td><td>Name each recurring error and add a check for it</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Step 5 feeds step 1. Each training run has more green glyphs than the last, so later drafts need fewer corrections.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Why local" label="Rules for machine output">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Rules for machine output</h2>
      <p class="t-body">
        A draft is a proposal, not a direct edit. Install and Undo use the
        ordinary proposal flow.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Only a person marks a glyph green. Machine drafts stay orange until
        they are graded.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Drafts land on the 8-unit grid. The designer makes the 2-unit optical
        corrections.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">
        Each engine is scored against drawn pairs. The baseline is a plain
        mean shift.
      </p>
      <p class="t-body">Nothing writes font files. The designer saves.</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The same rules apply to the local models, to agents over MCP, and to the Chat panel.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Part 1: demo ── -->

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Demo">
    <img
      class="bleed"
      src={endRunebender.src}
      alt="Runebender with a labeled bismillah and its preview."
    />
    <div class="c-1-9 over-bleed">
      <p class="t-display">Demo</p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Switch to Runebender. Order: open Shapes.ufo, train a shapes model from scratch, add a shape to fine-tune it, bolden octagon and chevron, ask the chat model about the font by voice, run the bolden graph in the Nodes tab.</p>
      <p>The slides that follow are reference for the same steps. Slides marked Planned describe features that are not finished.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Training from scratch">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Training from scratch</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Open a glyph, hold the shapes button, and choose Local AI Shapes.
        Without a model, the panel offers Train From the Sample Shapes.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        After training, choose a shape and drag a box. The model draws a
        preview in the box. Enter keeps it, Tab draws another, Esc removes it.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small muted">
        The model can draw only the shapes in the training font. Each glyph
        name is the word for its shape.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={outline}
      alt="Runebender editing an outline, with the right panel open."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>A quick training run takes about twelve minutes on a laptop GPU. A thorough run takes about thirty. get-base-model.sh in the demo repository downloads a trained base model with a checksum.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Fine-tuning">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Fine-tuning</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Fine-tuning continues training from an existing model instead of
        from random weights. It needs less data and less time.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        In the editor, draw a new shape and click Add This Font’s Shapes.
        Training continues from the current model for about two minutes.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        In font-ml, an adapter is a small set of weights trained over a
        frozen base model, in the shape of a LoRA. It is applied at load time
        with a strength.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">font-ml run train --source Regular.ufo --target Bold.ufo \
    --init my-bolden --adapter-out my-adapter --rank 8
font-ml run bolden --model my-bolden --source Regular.ufo \
    --glyph H --adapter my-adapter:1.0</pre>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Method</th><th>Starts from</th><th>Output</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Train from scratch</td><td>Random weights</td><td>A new model folder</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Continue training, --init</td><td>An existing model</td><td>A new model folder</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Adapter, --adapter-out</td><td>A frozen base model</td><td>adapter.json, adapter.safetensors</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Nodes tab includes a train-adapter graph that runs the same adapter training.</p>
      <p>Two adapters can be applied in a row. Both are merged into the weights at load time.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Fine-tuning in the editor" planned>
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Fine-tuning in the editor</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A Finetune button in the Local AI panel trains a bolden adapter on the
        glyphs graded green in the open font.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The panel shows whether each script has enough drawn pairs: 20 green
        pairs, with 5 held out for scoring.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The adapter is saved in <code>MyFont.models/</code> beside the font.
        Bolden drafts use it after training.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Draw and grade Bold glyphs green</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Click Finetune in the Local AI panel</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>An adapter trains with the base model frozen</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>The held-out pairs are scored with and without it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Bolden drafts use the adapter if its score is better</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Today the same adapter training runs from font-ml and from the train-adapter graph in the node editor. The button puts it in the panel for people who do not use the command line.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Bolden">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Bolden</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The model moves the points of a Regular glyph to make a Bold draft.
        The point structure stays the same, so the masters still interpolate.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Weight debt block lists Bold glyphs that are still identical to
        Regular. It drafts them in batches of 20 and marks them orange.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        About half a second per glyph on Metal.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 demo-side">
      <Fig
        class="fig-right"
        src={grid}
        alt="Runebender’s glyph overview grid for a Latin font."
      />
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Virtua Grotesk Bold</th><th>Owed</th><th>Drawn pairs</th><th>Offset vs model</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Latin</td><td>2</td><td>56</td><td>about even</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Arabic</td><td>11</td><td>130</td><td>offset is better</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hebrew</td><td>71</td><td>5</td><td>offset is better</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Measured 2026-10-09 on Virtua Grotesk. Both engines are only slightly better than the mean shift. Hebrew has five drawn pairs. An engine needs 20 green pairs on a script, with 5 held out, before its drafts are used.</p>
      <p>The score is the distance between the draft and the outline the designer kept.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Drafting from a sketch" planned>
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Drafting from a sketch</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The designer draws the rough shape of a glyph with the brush on the
        glyph canvas. The model returns a draft in the conventions of the
        font, as a proposal.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The chat model does not draw. A separate drawing model, trained on
        the glyphs graded green, redraws the traced sketch.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Step</th><th>Tool</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sketch the shape</td><td>Brush on the glyph canvas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trace the sketch</td><td>img2bez, raster to outline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Redraw in the font’s style</td><td>Drawing model, through font-ml</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Review</td><td>Proposal layer, Install or Discard</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Brush panel already traces temporary ink with img2bez and can draft with the Virtua sketch model. The first trial, on 2026-09-28, lost the form of the medial kaf. The drawing model is the open work, not the interface.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Open-weight chat models">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Open-weight chat models</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Open-weight models are published with their weights, so they run on
        your own machine. The Chat panel uses GGUF files, such as Qwen3 from
        Hugging Face.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Small models cannot calculate a stem width from a point list. They do
        call the correct tools in the correct order. The tools must return the
        measurement directly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Engine</th><th>Model</th><th>Time</th><th>Stem of H</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>font-ml chat (Candle)</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>543 s</td><td>74, wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>11 s</td><td>564, wrong: bounds width</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server</td><td>Qwen3 8B</td><td>24 s</td><td>Subtracted sidebearings, wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>llama-server + analyze_glyph</td><td>Qwen3 4B</td><td>5 s</td><td>100, correct</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Same question, same copy of Virtua Grotesk, M4 Pro. Two decisions came from this test: use llama.cpp as the engine, and make the tools do the measuring.</p>
      <p>A 0.6B model gives wrong answers. The Chat panel was built and tested with 4B.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Model catalog" planned>
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Model catalog</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Chat panel lists open-weight models with their size, license, and
        source. One click downloads a model from Hugging Face and checks its
        sha256 checksum. Nothing downloads without the click.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The editor runs the model with llama-server from llama.cpp. A GGUF
        file placed in a folder under <code>chat/</code> also works.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Entry</th><th>Model</th><th>Download</th><th>Machine</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Small, default</td><td>Qwen3.5 4B</td><td>about 2.7 GB</td><td>8 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Better</td><td>Qwen3.5 9B</td><td>about 5.5 GB</td><td>16 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Large</td><td>Qwen3.6 35B A3B</td><td>about 20 GB</td><td>32 GB or more</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Decided 2026-10-18: the catalog is shown in the app and the user picks. All three entries are Apache 2.0, 4-bit GGUF files. Gemma 4 and gpt-oss-20b can be added as optional entries.</p>
      <p>Thinking is off by default, with a switch in the composer.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="Voice input">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Voice input</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Talk button in the Chat panel records a spoken request. A local
        speech model transcribes it and puts the text in the field. The
        designer checks the text before sending.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        It uses whisper-server from whisper.cpp and a Whisper model in a
        folder whose name starts with <code>whisper</code>. The button is
        hidden until both are found.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-7-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">brew install whisper.cpp
~/Runebender/models/whisper-small/model.bin</pre>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The speech model is a whisper.cpp ggml file from huggingface.co/ggerganov/whisper.cpp, saved as model.bin. Audio stays on the machine.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Demo" label="The node editor">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">The node editor</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Runebender includes a node editor, similar to ComfyUI. Nodes for the
        font, a model, a master, and tasks such as Bolden, Compare, and
        Install connect into a workflow.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        A workflow is a file. It runs in the Nodes tab or from the command
        line.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        A run does not save or change the open font. Results are candidates
        until they are installed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fig
      class="c-5-12 fig-right"
      src={nodes}
      alt="Runebender’s Nodes tab with a workflow graph."
    />
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>External tasks declare their inputs, so unchanged runs are cached. Model folders are hashed from their contents, not from the manifest.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Part 2: hands-on ── -->

  <Slide section="Hands-on" label="Hands-on setup">
    <div class="c-1-10 stack">
      <h2 class="t-display">Hands-on setup</h2>
      <p class="t-body">
        Install Runebender and run a first local model on your own machine.
        The next slides cover what your hardware can run, how to install, and
        the exercises.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Hardware ── -->

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Memory size and bandwidth">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Memory size and bandwidth</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Memory size sets which models fit. At 4 bits, a model takes about
        0.6 GB per billion parameters, plus space for the context.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Memory bandwidth sets the speed. Each new token reads the active
        weights once, so tokens per second is at most bandwidth divided by
        their size.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Model</th><th>Parameters</th><th>File</th><th>Kind</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>qwen3.5-4b</td><td>4B</td><td>2.9 GB</td><td>dense</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gpt-oss-20b</td><td>21B</td><td>12.1 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 3.6B active</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gemma-4-26b-a4b</td><td>26B</td><td>17.0 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 4B active</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-27b</td><td>27B</td><td>17.6 GB</td><td>dense</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-35b-a3b</td><td>35B</td><td>22.4 GB</td><td>mixture of experts, 3B active</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>For people new to local models: the processor matters less than the size and speed of the memory the model can use.</p>
      <p>These are the chat models in my ~/Runebender/models folder, as 4-bit GGUF files. The file size is the memory the weights need.</p>
      <p>The operating system and the context also need memory. On a 16 GB machine, keep the model under about 10 GB.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Dense and mixture of experts">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Dense and mixture of experts</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A dense model reads every weight for every token. A mixture of experts
        reads only a few experts per token.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        qwen3.6-35b-a3b has 35B parameters and reads about 3B per token. It
        needs as much memory as a large model, but generates tokens about as
        fast as a small one.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>MacBook Pro, M4 Pro, 273 GB/s</th><th>Read per token</th><th>Upper limit</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-27b, dense</td><td>17.6 GB</td><td>about 15 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>qwen3.6-35b-a3b, mixture of experts</td><td>about 2 GB</td><td>about 130 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Measured on AMD Strix Halo, 128 GB</th><th>Speed</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Qwen3 30B-A3B, mixture of experts</td><td>about 100 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gpt-oss-120b, mixture of experts</td><td>about 55 tokens/s</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Llama 3.3 70B, dense</td><td>about 5 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The upper limit is bandwidth divided by the bytes read per token. Measured speed is lower. On the same machine, the mixture of experts is several times faster than the dense model.</p>
      <p>Many current open models are mixtures of experts, including gpt-oss and the Qwen3 A3B models. This is why machines with large unified memory are useful for local inference.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Three kinds of machine">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Graphics card</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        24 to 32 GB of fast memory on one consumer card.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        NVIDIA’s CUDA is supported by all inference and training tools. The
        card goes in a PC that can be built and upgraded.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-8 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Unified memory</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The processor and the graphics share one pool of 64 to 512 GB.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Lower bandwidth than a graphics card, but larger models fit. Macs, AMD
        Strix Halo, NVIDIA DGX Spark.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-9-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Multiple cards</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Several cards in one machine, or a workstation chip with datacenter
        memory.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        For labs and teams. tinybox, RTX Pro 6000 builds, DGX Station.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Inference works on all three. Training has the best support on NVIDIA. Small models, such as the Runebender font models, also train on Apple Metal.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Desktop machines up to 128 GB">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Desktop machines up to 128 GB</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Bandwidth</th><th>Price</th><th>Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>PC with a used RTX 3090</td><td>24 GB</td><td>936 GB/s</td><td>$700 to $1,050 per card</td><td>Lowest cost per GB, CUDA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PC with an RTX 5090</td><td>32 GB</td><td>1,792 GB/s</td><td>$2,400 to $4,300 per card</td><td>Fastest consumer card</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strix Halo mini PC</td><td>128 GB</td><td>256 GB/s</td><td>from about $2,000</td><td>120B mixtures of experts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Framework Desktop, Max+ 395</td><td>128 GB</td><td>256 GB/s</td><td>$3,449</td><td>Same chip, repairable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mac Studio, M5 Max</td><td>36 to 128 GB</td><td>460 to 614 GB/s</td><td>from $2,499</td><td>MLX and llama.cpp, low noise</td></tr>
          <tr><td>NVIDIA DGX Spark</td><td>64 or 128 GB</td><td>273 GB/s</td><td>$3,999 at launch, now higher</td><td>CUDA, two units link</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="t-credit">
        US prices, October 2026, from vendor pages and retail listings. Memory
        shortages raised many of these prices during 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Strix Halo is the AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395. GMKtec, HP, Beelink, Minisforum and Framework sell machines with the same chip.</p>
      <p>The DGX Spark is the only machine in this table with both CUDA and 128 GB. Its bandwidth is close to Strix Halo’s. The extra cost buys the NVIDIA software stack and the option to link two units for 256 GB.</p>
      <p>A 64 GB DGX Spark from OEM partners started at $4,999 in October 2026.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Larger machines">
