  <Slide section="Hardware" label="A Linux PC">
    <div class="c-1-6 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">A Linux PC</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A common starting point is one used RTX 3090: 24 GB at 936 GB/s for
        about $1,000. A second card brings the total to 48 GB. llama.cpp
        splits a model across cards.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The graphics card does the work. The other parts supply power, space,
        and data.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Most training tools are developed and tested on Linux with CUDA first.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-7-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Part</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Graphics card</td><td>Memory first, then bandwidth</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power supply</td><td>1000 W or more for two cards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Motherboard</td><td>Space for two wide cards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>System memory</td><td>64 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Disk</td><td>2 TB NVMe for model files</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Processor</td><td>Any recent desktop processor</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>An RTX 3090 draws about 350 W. Two cards need a large power supply and good airflow.</p>
      <p>AMD cards work with llama.cpp through Vulkan and ROCm. Training tools support them less well.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="My setup">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">My setup</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Runebender font models are one to a few dozen megabytes. A
        consumer graphics card is enough to train them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Machine</th><th>Memory</th><th>Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>MacBook Pro, M4 Pro</td><td>48 GB, 273 GB/s</td><td>Chat and coding, qwen3.6-35b-a3b</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linux PC, RTX 2060 SUPER</td><td>8 GB, 448 GB/s</td><td>Training, about 100 s per epoch</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Steam Deck</td><td>16 GB shared</td><td>qwen3.5-4b at 18 tokens/s</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Linux PC trained the Nasta’liq model in the Nasta’liq Distilled talk. The Steam Deck runs Runebender and a chat model with the Vulkan build of llama.cpp.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Runtimes">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Runtimes</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        A runtime loads a model file and runs it. Most runtimes can also serve
        the model on a local address that other programs call.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The Runebender Chat panel connects to llama.cpp this way.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Runtime</th><th>Description</th><th>Hardware</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>llama.cpp</td><td>Runs GGUF files, the common format</td><td>Mac, NVIDIA, AMD, CPU</td></tr>
          <tr><td>MLX</td><td>Apple’s machine learning library</td><td>Mac</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ollama, LM Studio</td><td>Desktop apps built on the two above</td><td>Mac, Windows, Linux</td></tr>
          <tr><td>vLLM</td><td>Serves many users at once</td><td>NVIDIA servers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PyTorch, Candle</td><td>Training; font-ml uses Candle</td><td>NVIDIA, Mac</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Ollama and LM Studio are the simplest first step outside Runebender: install the app, choose a model, and start a chat.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Hardware" label="Choosing a machine">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Choosing a machine</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Goal</th><th>Starting point</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>This workshop</td><td>The computer you have. A 4B chat model needs 3 GB.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Train small font models</td><td>A Mac with Apple silicon, or an NVIDIA card with 8 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30B models at high speed</td><td>A 24 to 32 GB NVIDIA card, or a Mac with 48 GB or more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>120B models on a desktop</td><td>128 GB of unified memory: Strix Halo, Mac Studio, DGX Spark</td></tr>
          <tr><td>The largest open models</td><td>Mac Studio, M5 Ultra, 256 to 512 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Teams, or training large models</td><td>RTX Pro 6000 builds, tinybox, DGX Station</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Start with the hardware you have. Buy new hardware when a real task is too slow, and choose by memory first.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Setup ── -->

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Installation" planned>
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Installation</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Mac: download the DMG from runebender.org and drag Runebender to
        Applications.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Mac and Linux, to build or modify the editor: install with Cargo,
        using stable Rust from rustup.rs. On Linux, install the system
        libraries first.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        <code>font-ml</code> runs and trains the models. Build it with
        <code>metal</code> on a Mac, <code>cuda</code> with NVIDIA, or no
        feature for CPU.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code"># Debian or Ubuntu
sudo apt-get install libfontconfig-dev libwayland-dev \
    libxkbcommon-dev libx11-dev libxcb1-dev libvulkan1 \
    libssl-dev libglib2.0-dev libgtk-3-dev libxdo-dev \
    libasound2-dev</pre>
      <pre class="code">cargo install --git https://github.com/fontgarden/runebender
cargo install --git https://github.com/eliheuer/font-ml \
    --features metal     # or cuda, or no feature for CPU</pre>
      <pre class="code">runebender --help
font-ml tasks</pre>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The Linux package list is the one the Runebender CI installs on Ubuntu.</p>
      <p>font-ml tasks lists the available tasks. Only tasks marked ready can be used.</p>
      <p>Compiling takes about fifteen minutes. Start the build before Part 1 if you can.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Setup with a coding agent">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Setup with a coding agent</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Claude Code, Codex, or another coding agent can do the setup. Give it
        the setup instructions published on runebender.org.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        The instructions tell the agent to inspect the machine first, ask
        before each download or install, leave your own fonts unchanged, and
        report what it did.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">Read https://runebender.org/local-ai-setup-prompt.md
and set up Runebender for local AI on this machine.</pre>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Step</th><th>What the agent does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Inspect</td><td>Reports the OS, disk space, Rust, and existing tools</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Install</td><td>runebender, and font-ml for the right GPU</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Verify</td><td>runebender --help and font-ml tasks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Train</td><td>The first shapes model, or downloads the base</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Report</td><td>Platform, device, training time, models</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The chat model download is optional, about 800 MB. The agent does not open the editor unless asked.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Model folders">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Model folders</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        Models are read from <code>~/Runebender/models/&lt;kind&gt;/</code>, or
        from <code>MyFont.models/</code> beside the font, so the model stays
        with the font.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        Each kind is a different type of model. A chat model cannot draw, and
        a shapes model cannot chat.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12">
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kind</th><th>Files</th><th>Source</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>shapes</td><td>config, weights, vocab, manifest</td><td>Local AI Shapes tool, from a UFO</td></tr>
          <tr><td>bolden</td><td>config, weights, vocab</td><td>font-ml run train, from two masters</td></tr>
          <tr><td>chat</td><td>model.gguf, tokenizer.json</td><td>Download; the demo script gets Qwen3</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The editor reads only these files. The config is plain JSON.</p>
      <p>RUNEBENDER_MODELS sets a different model root for one run.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="Setup" label="Exercises">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Exercises</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        The demo repository has a small font family made for training,
        scripts, and node graphs. Work through the numbered folders in order.
        Each has a README.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        To reset the fonts after the exercises, run
        <code>git checkout -- fonts</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">git clone https://github.com/eliheuer/runebender-local-ai-demo
cd runebender-local-ai-demo
runebender fonts/Shapes.ufo</pre>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Folder</th><th>Exercise</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>01-draw-from-text</td><td>Train a shapes model from scratch, then fine-tune it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>02-train-and-bolden</td><td>Train a bolden model and bolden a new shape</td></tr>
          <tr><td>03-chat-model</td><td>Download an open-weight chat model and ask about the font</td></tr>
          <tr><td>04-nodes</td><td>The same steps as a workflow in the node editor</td></tr>
          <tr><td>05-real-font</td><td>The bolden loop on Virtua Grotesk</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>Exercise 01 is the one to finish if there is time for only one.</p>
      <p>If training fails, 01-draw-from-text/get-base-model.sh downloads a trained base model with a checksum.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ── Agents ── -->

  <Slide section="Agents" label="Agents over MCP">
    <div class="c-1-4 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Agents over MCP</h2>
      <p class="t-small">
        One command registers the editor with Claude Code, Codex, Claude
        Desktop and Cursor. The agent works on the open document, including
        unsaved changes.
      </p>
      <p class="t-small">
        45 tools: read, measure, proof, propose point edits, and fork
        experiments. Five workflows as prompts: review-glyph,
        compatibility-check, spacing-pass, kerning-pass, draft-weight.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-5-12 stack">
      <pre class="code">runebender mcp --install</pre>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kind of AI</th><th>Access to the font</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Local models</td><td>font-ml tasks in the editor: shapes, bolden, brush</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agent in the repository</td><td>Edits GLIF files, plists, scripts, CI</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Agent in the editor</td><td>runebender mcp --live, proposals and receipts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Vision model on proofs</td><td>Ranks drafts for review; does not grade</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Local chat</td><td>The Chat panel, without an external agent</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <p>The editor is designed to be extended with both cloud and local agents. The rules for machine output apply here too. The agent reads first and proofs the result. The designer installs.</p>
      <p>Figma and Penpot have MCP servers, Glyphs has a third-party one, and FontLab 9 has an in-app chat. Runebender adds a review step (proof, compare, grade) and a safety contract (proposals, receipts, no saves).</p>
      <p>A model does not have to run inside Runebender. Any Python model can read the UFO and submit proposals through MCP.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Slide>

  <Slide section="End" label="Planned work">
    <div class="c-1-12 stack">
      <h2 class="t-head">Planned work</h2>
      <table class="mono-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr>
